# SRG-APP-000220-WSR-000201  -  CAT II
# The web server must invalidate session identifiers upon hosted application
# user logout or other session termination.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000220-WSR-000201
group:           V-206396
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-001185
control_rev5:    SC-23

contribution:    Splunk Enterprise can invalidate the tokens associated with a logged-out
                 session, delete the session cookie when the browser quits rather than
                 persisting it across restarts, and give authorization tokens a finite
                 expiration so no issued identifier outlives the session indefinitely.
                 Cross-node propagation of a logout takes effect only where search head
                 clustering and the App Key Value store are in use.

governs:         authorize.conf - Used to create roles.
governs:         server.conf - Core server settings, including the certificates and protocols used to
                 protect management and replication traffic.
governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.restart_persist not_equals true
check:           authorize.conf [tokens_auth] expiration not_equals never
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          authorize.conf [tokens_auth] expiration https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.3-configuration-file-reference/authorize.conf
vendor:          server.conf [general] invalidateSessionTokensOnLogout https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/server.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.restart_persist https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Propagation of a logout across nodes, which the spec states takes effect
                 only when search head clustering and the App Key Value store are enabled -
                 the deployment architecture
external:        Deletion of the session cookie in the user's browser once the server has
                 invalidated it - client browser
external:        Invalidation of sessions held by a fronting SSO proxy or identity provider
                 at logout - that component's single logout configuration
external:        Cluster-wide invalidation of session tokens at logout, which server.conf
                 [general] invalidateSessionTokensOnLogout performs only on a search head
                 cluster member with the KV store enabled; this instance has search head
                 clustering disabled, so the setting asserts a protection the product would
                 not apply.

citation:        server.conf.spec:81-87 (invalidateSessionTokensOnLogout = <boolean>,
                 invalidates tokens associated with a logged-out session, default false;
                 effective only with search head clustering and App Key Value store
                 enabled); web.conf.spec:934-940 (tools.sessions.restart_persist =
                 <boolean>; false deletes the session cookie when the browser quits);
                 authorize.conf.spec:381 ([tokens_auth]) with :390-409 (expiration =
                 <relative-time-modifier>|never, default +30d)
finding:         Session identifiers are not invalidated on termination to the extent the
                 product allows: logout token invalidation is not enabled, the session
                 cookie is configured to persist across browser restarts, or authorization
                 tokens are set never to expire.
